.TH UPAS-SEND 1
.SH NAME
upas/send \- submit mail through an SMTP server over TLS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B upas/send
[
.B -s
.I server
] [
.B -a
.I dialip
] [
.B -p
.I port
] [
.B -u
.I user
] [
.B -P
.I passfile
] [
.B -f
.I from
] [
.B -t
.I rcpt
] ... [
.I msgfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Upas/send
submits a message through an SMTP server over
.IR tls12 (2).
It defaults to Gmail
.RB ( smtp.gmail.com :465).
.PP
With no
.B -u
it only probes the transport: it completes the TLS handshake, reads the
greeting, prints the
.B EHLO
capabilities and exits, sending no mail.
.PP
With
.B -u
.IR user ,
.B -P
.IR passfile ,
.B -f
.I from
and one or more
.B -t
.I rcpt
it authenticates with the password from
.I passfile
(for Gmail, an
.IR "app password" ),
then sends the message read from
.I msgfile
(or standard input).
The message should be a complete RFC 822 message \(em headers, a blank
line, then the body.
.PP
As with
.IR upas-fetch (1),
a hosted
.I emu
does not resolve names, so give the already-resolved address with
.B -a
while the certificate is verified for the
.B -s
name, and bind
.B "#I"
onto
.B /net
first.
.SH EXAMPLES
Probe Gmail's submission endpoint (sends nothing):
.EX

	bind -a '#I' /net
	upas/send -s smtp.gmail.com -a 142.251.16.108

.EE
Send a message with an app password:
.EX

	upas/send -u me@gmail.com -P /tmp/gpass -f me@gmail.com \e
		-t you@example.com -a 142.251.16.108 message.txt

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/upas/send.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR smtp2 (2),
.IR upas-fetch (1),
.IR tls12 (2)
.SH BUGS
Authentication is
.B "AUTH PLAIN"
only (an app password), not XOAUTH2.
